\n3. Reduced latency: On-device processing means no delay in responses. For instance, voice assistants can respond immediately without waiting for a cloud server.\n\n4. Battery efficiency: Local processing doesn't consume as much battery as cloud services. Cloud models might require more power, especially if they're running continuously.\n\n5. Custom"}}, {"text": "", "channels": {"thought": "Okay, let's see. The user wants me to list 25 distinct things that on-device AI on a phone can do that cloud models can't. And each item needs two full sentences explaining it. They also mentioned not to stop early, so I need to make sure I get all 25.\n\nFirst, I need to recall what on-device AI is. It's AI that runs on the phone's hardware, not the cloud. So things like local processing, real-time performance, data privacy, etc. Let me think of possible points.\n\n1. Real-time processing: On-device AI can process data instantly without sending to the cloud. For example, image recognition in real-time, like a camera app. Cloud models might take longer or require internet.\n\n2. Privacy and data security: Since data stays on the device, it's harder for third parties to access. Cloud models might have data sent to servers, which could be intercepted.\n\n3. Reduced latency: On-device processing means no delay in responses. For instance, voice assistants can respond immediately without waiting for a cloud server.\n\n4. Battery efficiency: Local processing doesn't consume as much battery as cloud services. Cloud models might require more power, especially if they're running continuously.\n\n5. Custom"}}], "versions": {"python": "3.14.8", "platform": "android", "machine": "aarch64", "litert-lm-api": "0.19.0"}, "lib": {"path": "/data/local/tmp/s29q3_0190/kpy/wheel/litert_lm/liblitert-lm.so", "bytes": 22656360, "sha256": "28fa8433febc49a99f4bd3ef8cb82c2912457cfe274d97944debeabdeb97fc78"}, "litert_lm_file": "/data/local/tmp/s29q3_0190/kpy/wheel/litert_lm/__init__.py", "model": "/data/lo